  "text": "Recipe: Purple Cabbage Patties\nCreator: Kiran Sharma\nCategory: Patties\nIngredients: 2 cups shredded purple cabbage, 1/2 cup grated carrots, 1/2 cup cooked purple rice, 1/2 cup tiger nut flour, 1/2 medium sized cooked purple sweet potato, 1 Tbsp ground flaxseeds, 3 Tbsp water, 2 cloves garlic, minced, 1/2 jalape\u00f1o, 2 spring onions with greens, 1 tsp onion powder, 1 tsp smoked paprika, 1 tsp red chilli powder, 1/2 tsp ground cumin, 1 tsp Garam Masala Powder, Himalayan Salt and pepper, to taste, 2 Tbsp chopped fresh coriander leaves, Ezekiel 4:9 bread crumbs\nCooking methods: no-cook, bake, broil\nInstructions: FLAX EGG: In a small bowl, mix ground flaxseeds with water to create flax eggs. Let it sit for a few minutes until thickened.\nBATTER: In a large mixing bowl combine everything except for the bread crumbs. Combine the cabbage mixture lightly with your hands to avoid drawing too much water from the cabbage. Mix until the mixture is cohesive.\nPATTIES: Scoop portions of the mixture and shape into patties using your hands. Coat the patties with the Ezekiel 4:9 breadcrumbs Repeat until all the mixture is used.\nBAKE: Place the patties in a parchment lined baking tray. Bake the patties in a preheated oven at 375\u00b0F for 35-40 minutes, flipping halfway through. After baking them I quickly broiled them until golden and crispy.\nTIPS: (1) If the cabbage is too moist, squeeze out excess water to prevent soggy fritters. (2) Instead of purple rice, brown rice or quinoa may be used. (3) Tiger nut flour may be substituted by cassava flour or chickpea flour.\nNotes: (1) If the cabbage is too moist, squeeze out excess water to prevent soggy fritters. (2) Instead of purple rice, brown rice or quinoa may be used. (3) Tiger nut flour may be substituted by cassava flour or chickpea flour.",
